const SELLER_CREDIT_GUIDE=[["Conventional (Conforming/High Balance)", "2–9%, LTV-tiered", "≤75% LTV: 9% · 75.01–90%: 6% · >90%: 3% (primary/second). Investment property: 2% regardless of LTV."], ["FHA / 203(k)", "6%", "Toward closing costs & prepaids only — never toward the down payment or reserves."], ["VA / Jumbo VA", "4% concessions + unlimited costs", "4% cap applies to true \"concessions\" (payoff of debt, cash to vet); actual closing costs/prepaids/discount points are separately unlimited."], ["USDA", "6%", "Toward closing costs/prepaids; guarantee fee may also be financed."], ["CalHFA / CalVet / DPA-layered", "Follows base first-mortgage program", "DPA/silent-second is separate from the seller-credit cap on the first mortgage."], ["Jumbo (Conventional)", "Lender-specific, typically 2–9% LTV-tiered", "Many jumbo investors mirror conventional tiers but confirm per-lender overlay."], ["Non-QM (Bank Statement, DSCR, Foreign National, ITIN, No-Income-Verification)", "Lender-specific, typically 2–6%", "No agency rule — set entirely by the individual Non-QM investor's guidelines; confirm before disclosing."], ["Hard Money / Fix N Flip / Bridge", "Rarely allowed", "Most private/hard-money lenders don't permit seller credits — verify per deal."]];
const STRATEGY_TIPS=["Fund a 2-1 or 3-2-1 temporary rate buydown with seller credit instead of (or alongside) closing-cost credit — lowers the client's payment in years 1–2/3 when budgets are tightest, without permanently buying down the rate.", "Layer a CalHFA/county DPA silent second on top of an FHA or conventional first when the client is short on down payment but otherwise well-qualified — check income/purchase-price limits and required homebuyer education early since DPA approval can lag the first mortgage.", "For self-employed clients whose tax returns understate income, run Bank Statement AND DSCR (if it's a rental) side by side — compare qualifying income and rate hit before picking a program; sometimes 12-month bank statement beats 24-month once the expense factor is negotiated down with a CPA letter.", "On a VA deal, remember the 4% concession cap doesn't include seller-paid closing costs, prepaids, or discount points — structure the offer to maximize seller-paid costs outside the 4% bucket before touching the concession cap.", "For a veteran with full entitlement and strong reserves, consider Jumbo VA (up to 89.99% LTV, no monthly MI, to ~$3M) instead of routing to conventional jumbo with MI — a significant payment advantage in San Diego/coastal price bands.", "Use a temporary or permanent buydown funded by the LISTING agent's commission (agent-paid buydown) when the seller won't budge but the agent will, especially on a slower-moving listing.", "For a client who is house-hacking (buying a 2–4 unit and living in one unit), use the subject property's projected rental income to help qualify — FHA/VA both allow a percentage of market rent from the other units toward qualifying income.", "For asset-rich, income-light clients (retirees, trust beneficiaries), use asset depletion / asset utilization qualifying (liquid assets divided by a term, e.g. 360 months) instead of forcing a traditional income calculation — several Non-QM shops (LendSure, HomeXpress) offer this natively.", "When a client's condo building fails warrantability review, don't default to Non-QM immediately — some agency lenders will still do a Limited Review on a second home/investment purchase under 75% LTV; check that first since it's cheaper than Non-QM.", "For a self-employed borrower who can't season 12–24 months of bank statements yet, consider a 1-year tax return exception (some agency AUS findings allow 1-year self-employment history with 2+ years in the same field) before defaulting to Non-QM.", "Structure a bridge loan against the departing residence's equity so the client can write a non-contingent offer on the next home — materially strengthens the offer in a competitive listing, then pay off the bridge at the old-home closing.", "For a physician client early in residency/fellowship, a signed future-employment contract (starting within 60–90 days) can count as qualifying income under most Doctors Loan programs — don't wait for pay stubs to start the file.", "For a client just under a program's FICO cutoff, order a rapid rescore before quoting a rate — often a $50–150 rescore fee clears a paid-down revolving balance or a disputed tradeline fast enough to jump a full underwriting tier.", "On a DACA file, confirm the EAD card isn't within 90 days of expiration at closing — a lapse mid-process is the single most common DACA-file killer; renew first if it's close.", "For a non-warrantable condo or manufactured home that keeps getting declined, ask whether a portfolio lender (Advancial FCU, First National Bank of America) will hold the loan in-house — portfolio lenders can waive agency project-approval requirements entirely.", "When a listing has multiple offers, a fully-underwritten pre-approval (not just pre-qual) with a short, clean contingency window is often worth more to a seller than a slightly higher price — walk agents through that ahead of the deal, not after."];
